--TEST--
`Core\Arr::from` draining a sequence with no `limit` is stopped by `[limits] memory` inside the drain, so the array it was building never reaches the program — `rule:programs/memory-priority`, `rule:errors/on-limit`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:iteration/materialising-a-sequence-core-arr-from
// The drive is native: between two elements the request passes no statement
// boundary and calls no member, so the ask `crates/nvs-runtime/src/sequence.rs`
// makes per element is the only thing in the loop that reads the balance.
// Unbounded, this grows until the host runs out and takes every other request
// on the worker with it — `limit` is the caller's bound, and a program that
// arrived with a request passes what it likes.
class Endless {
    // Bounded at four million for the reason the sibling ceiling case gives:
    // sixty-four megabytes of elements is four times the ceiling, so the bound
    // is unreachable in a run this case passes, and a red case fails rather
    // than taking the suite's host with it.
    public static function counter(): Iterator<int> {
        int $i = 0;
        while ($i < 4000000) {
            yield $i;
            $i = $i + 1;
        }
    }
}

// `--EXPECT--` is exact, so this line being the whole of stdout is what asserts
// the drain was stopped where it grew: the `echo` under it is reached only by a
// call that came back with an array.
echo "before\n";
array<int> $everything = Core\Arr::from(Endless::counter());
echo "kept ", Core\Arr::count($everything), "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The ceiling the drain above crosses, and nothing else.
[limits]
memory = "16M"
--EXPECT--
before
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
FATAL: the request exceeded its memory limit — %d bytes held against a ceiling of 16777216, of which 1048576 is reserved for the limit handler
